Subject: [PATCH] PM: hibernate: read frozen tasks' anonymous memory in place

SNAPSHOT_INPLACE (after SNAPSHOT_FREEZE) marks the anonymous pages of every
address space whose users are all frozen tasks, and takes a reference on each
folio it marks. The atomic copy skips them and snapshot_read_next() hands them
out from where they are, so making an image needs free memory only for the
kernel, page cache and other non-anonymous pages. The image format is
unchanged; the restoring kernel needs nothing new.

What is never read in place, and is copied as before:
- an address space with a task that is not frozen (an OOM victim, an
  io_uring or vhost worker that did not freeze), a kernel thread using it,
  or an mmu notifier (KVM, RDMA ODP, HMM, SVA: secondary MMUs that write
  without holding a page reference);
- a folio holding any reference besides its mappings and the swap cache:
  a DMA or GUP pin (io_uring fixed buffers, O_DIRECT in flight, RDMA,
  AF_XDP, vfio), a GUP get, one isolated by reclaim or migration;
- KSM, hugetlb, zone-device, hwpoisoned pages, VM_IO and VM_PFNMAP mappings.

A kernel thread that takes up an address space (kthread_use_mm) while pages
are held in place may write one, so from then on reading the image fails
with -EIO and the caller thaws instead of sending a torn image.

In place, preallocation never shrinks memory and allocates without I/O:
shrinking would write the marked pages to swap without freeing them, and the
caller may already have suspended the swap device. An image that doesn't fit
in free memory fails with -ENOMEM instead. SNAPSHOT_UNFREEZE, SNAPSHOT_FREE,
closing the device and every swsusp_free() outside the atomic section drop
the references; the resumed kernel drops its copy of them the same way.

